What is HTTPS?
HTTPS (HyperText Transfer Protocol Secure) is the secure version of HTTP, the protocol your web browser uses to communicate with websites.

The main difference is that HTTPS encrypts the data sent between your browser and the website, making it much more secure.

When you visit an HTTPS website:

Your browser connects to the website.
The website presents a SSL/TLS certificate to prove its identity.
Your browser verifies the certificate.
An encrypted connection is established.
All data exchanged (such as passwords, credit card numbers, and messages) is encrypted.

HTTPS provides three key security benefits:

Encryption: Prevents others from reading the data being transmitted.
Authentication: Helps ensure you're communicating with the legitimate website, not an imposter.
Data integrity: Protects data from being modified while in transit.

Difference between SSL and TLS.
SSL and TLS are security protocols used to protect network communication. TLS is the successor to SSL and provides improved security, stronger cryptographic algorithms, better handshake mechanisms, and modern features such as forward secrecy. SSL 2.0 and SSL 3.0 are obsolete and should not be used. Modern HTTPS uses TLS, primarily TLS 1.2 or TLS 1.3.

